{
  "id": "T18_54_a_transition_caused_by_several_records_at_one_instant_cites_every_one",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.6.6",
    "7.6.4",
    "7.6.5",
    "6.5.1",
    "7.2.6",
    "9.3.1",
    "9.5.1",
    "12.19"
  ],
  "description": "Door 201's finish conflict begins at the architect's 50% CD, where A-601 and the specification both first state it: the begin act cites both Assertions, in identifier order. Both are reissued at 100% CD with the same finish: the end act cites both. For Door 202 only A-601 changes at 100% CD, the specification restating its value: A-601's Assertion alone ended the story under either order, and the end act cites it alone. Two of the architect's communications published in one IFC issue both meet a party requirement: the fulfillment cites both, as fulfilled_by and as the end act's record. Under the fixture policy, a directive whose two requirements are fulfilled at the same issue closes its change story with directive_closed citing both ending records.",
  "note": "7.6.6: \"When several records cause one transition, being at one instant with no sequence evidence ordering them (4.5.2), and every order of them gives the transition the same time and cause, the transition cites all of them: `record` is the list of their exact references, ordered by `item` id, then `context` id, then `source` id ... A transition caused by one record gives that record alone, not a list.\" 6.5.1: \"where several earliest share one occurrence time with no sequence evidence ordering them (4.5.2), it references all of them (7.6.6)\". README section 5, Ending policies: for directive_closed, \"where several ended last at one instant, it is the list of all their ending records (7.6.6)\". The step writing A-601's Door 201 Assertion at 100% CD before the specification's leaves its `generated` unpinned: whether that write records a continuation is not what this fixture tests. The 100% CD milestone is published before both writes so that each write is placed when it is made.",
